---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * Sibling of fitnotes-alternative.astro. Read that file's header first; the
 * trademark rules and the source-checking rule are identical.
 *
 * NO CLAIMS ABOUT THEIR PRICING OR THEIR FEATURE SET. Competitor pricing moves,
 * and a page that is wrong about it is worse than a page that never mentioned
 * it. Everything comparative here is a statement about what BADGER does not
 * charge for, which we can keep true.
 *
 * Import claims checked against strong_parser.dart and strong_importer.dart on
 * 2026-09-05:
 *   - delimiter sniffed from the header line, ';' or ',', for the European
 *     exports (strong_parser.dart:61)
 *   - weight unit read from the 'Weight (kg)' / 'Weight (lbs)' header, not
 *     guessed (:91)
 *   - carried: Set Order, Reps, Distance, Seconds, Notes (per set), Workout
 *     Notes, Workout Name, RPE (:96-107, importer :242-243)
 *   - session start/end resolved from Strong's full datetime Date column via
 *     resolveSessionTimes (:134)
 *   - NOT carried: warmups. Strong's export does not mark them, and nothing in
 *     the parser infers them from Set Order. Do not add a warmup claim here.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from Strong without losing your log. What the CSV export carries across, including your RPE and per-set notes, and what none of it costs.';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from Strong" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from Strong"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-05"
    about={['Strong', 'Workout tracking']}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from Strong</h1>

  <p>
    Strong is a good app, and if it suits you there is no reason to move. People usually land here
    for one of two reasons: they would rather not rent their own training log, or they want it to
    live on the phone rather than in an account. This page covers what survives the move and what you
    trade.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the CSV carries across</h2>

  <p>
    Strong exports a CSV. Badger reads it on your phone, with nothing uploaded, and it takes more of
    it than most importers bother with.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, exercise, weight, reps, distance, and time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your RPE.</strong> If you logged effort, it comes across on the set rather than being
      dropped, so a decade of "how hard was that" is still there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Per-set notes, kept on their set.</strong> The note you left on one heavy triple stays
      attached to that triple. Workout notes and the workout's name become notes on the session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real session times.</strong> Strong's date column is a full timestamp, so imported
      workouts show when they started and how long they ran instead of showing nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kilograms or pounds</strong>, read from the file's own header rather than guessed, and
      stored in the unit you logged in. Nothing is converted or rounded on the way in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The European export.</strong> Strong writes a semicolon-separated file in some locales.
      Badger sniffs the delimiter from the header line, so that file works without you doing
      anything to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PRs, recalculated</strong> from the imported history, so your records and graphs start
      out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not, so nothing surprises you later:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups, as warmups.</strong> The Strong export does not mark them, so every logged set
      arrives as a working set. Nothing can tell retroactively which ones were ramp-up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your routines.</strong> The export is history, not programs. Badger ships seven program
      templates and you can build your own, but the routines themselves are set up again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise categories.</strong> A CSV carries none, so new exercises land in an
      "Imported" category for you to re-sort. Anything already in your Badger library is matched by
      name rather than duplicated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>In Strong, go to <strong>Profile &rarr; Settings &rarr; Export Data</strong> to produce a <code>strong.csv</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Get the file onto the phone you train with, however you normally move a file: email it to
      yourself, drop it in cloud storage, use a cable.
    </li>
    <li>In Badger, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from Strong</strong> and pick the file.</li>
    <li>Check the preview: exercises, workout days, sets, and the detected weight unit.</li>
    <li>Import.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    It runs more than once safely. Sets landing on a date you already have merge into that session,
    and nothing already logged is overwritten. Full detail in
    <a href="/help/getting-started#importing-strong">importing from Strong</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What you get, and what you give up</h2>

  <p>
    Worth being straight about both, because the honest version is more useful than a feature list.
  </p>

  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account and no sync.</strong> Your log is on your phone. Nothing to sign into,
      nothing to be locked out of, no server that can change the deal later.
      <a href="/promise">The promise</a> is that written down.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing behind a paywall.</strong> No cap on routines, on history, or on graphs, and
      exporting your own numbers is not a paid feature.
      <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription">The no-subscription guide</a> goes through
      what free actually means across the trackers people weigh up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking</strong>, with PRs scoped to the machine, because the leg
      press at one gym is not the leg press at another. <a href="/help/gyms">How it works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator and warmup generator</strong> that work from the plates your gym
      actually owns, and <a href="/help/progress">graphs</a> with estimated 1RM, muscle balance and
      plateau detection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What you give up:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloud sync between devices.</strong> Badger is local-first by design. Moving to a new
      phone is a backup file you carry over, which is
      <a href="/help/phone-migration">a documented path</a>, not a background service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A social feed.</strong> There is nobody to follow and nothing to post. If that was part
      of what kept you training, it is a real loss and worth weighing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    The reason to trust an import is knowing you can leave. Badger exports every set to CSV and backs
    the whole database up to a <a href="/badger-format">documented file format</a> that is ordinary
    readable text. Neither needs an account, and neither is a paid feature.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Strong is a trademark of its owner, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by,
    or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility and nothing else: Badger reads
    the CSV file that their app exports.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
